---
// The one study on the poster (v10_B_23): wheat sown in the Early against the Main window, NVT pairs.
// Text and numbers are the poster's. The R script under public/code/ re-runs the main analysis
// from the open data; its headline (src/data/study/c2_script_headline.csv) is shown beside the poster's.
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { href } from "../../data/site";
import code from "../../../public/code/r_code_c2_sowing_window_v1_24Oct2026.R?raw";
import headCsv from "../../data/study/c2_script_headline.csv?raw";

const fileCode = "r_code_c2_sowing_window_v1_24Oct2026.R";
const script = Object.fromEntries(headCsv.trim().split("\n").slice(1).map((l) => {
  const m = l.match(/^(?:"([^"]*)"|([^,]*)),(?:"([^"]*)"|(.*))$/);
  if (!m) throw new Error(`c2_script_headline.csv: unreadable line ${l}`);
  return [m[1] ?? m[2], m[3] ?? m[4]];
}));
// poster figure against the script's output, quantity by quantity
for (const k of ["pairs, sites (study set)", "days apart: min, median, max", "Main-window mean, t/ha", "average gain, t/ha (90% interval)",
  "share of the Main-window mean", "north gain, t/ha (90% interval)", "south gain, t/ha (90% interval)", "chance of a gain on average",
  "chance of a gain in the north", "chance of a gain, new paddock and season", "negative-control pairs, gain t/ha (90% interval)",
  "price, $/t", "value, $/ha (90% interval)"])
  if (!(k in script)) throw new Error(`c2_script_headline.csv: no row "${k}"`);
const compare = [
  ["Pairs and sites", "205 pairs at 46 sites", script["pairs, sites (study set)"].replace(", ", " pairs at ") + " sites"],
  ["Days apart", "7 to 52 (median 19)", (() => { const [a, b, c] = script["days apart: min, median, max"].split(", "); return `${a} to ${c} (median ${b})`; })()],
  ["Main-window mean", "3.8 t/ha", `${script["Main-window mean, t/ha"]} t/ha`],
  ["Average gain", "0.22 t/ha (0.06 to 0.39)", `${script["average gain, t/ha (90% interval)"]}`.replace(" (", " t/ha (")],
  ["Share of the Main-window mean", "6%", script["share of the Main-window mean"]],
  ["Gain in the north", "0.12 t/ha (-0.06 to 0.30)", script["north gain, t/ha (90% interval)"].replace(" (", " t/ha (")],
  ["Gain in southern NSW", "0.31 t/ha", script["south gain, t/ha (90% interval)"].replace(" (", " t/ha (")],
  ["Chance of a gain on average", "98%", script["chance of a gain on average"]],
  ["Chance of a gain in the north", "87%", script["chance of a gain in the north"]],
  ["Chance of a gain, new paddock and season", "65%", script["chance of a gain, new paddock and season"]],
  ["Pairs sown under a week apart", "82 pairs: 0.05 t/ha (-0.03 to 0.13)", script["negative-control pairs, gain t/ha (90% interval)"].replace(" (", " t/ha (")],
  ["Wheat price, study seasons", "$261/t", `$${script["price, $/t"]}/t`],
  ["Value of the average gain", "$58/ha ($15 to $103)", (() => { const m = script["value, $/ha (90% interval)"].match(/(\d+) \((\d+) to (\d+)\)/); return m ? `$${m[1]}/ha ($${m[2]} to $${m[3]})` : script["value, $/ha (90% interval)"]; })()],
];
---
<Base title="Does wheat sown earlier yield more?" description="Same-paddock pairs of NVT wheat trials sown in the Early and the Main window, NSW and Queensland, 2008 to 2017: 0.22 t/ha more when sown early, worth $58/ha." path="/examples/sowing-time">
  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="kicker">The study · open NVT results through DataHub</p>
      <h1>Does wheat sown earlier yield more?</h1>
      <p class="prose lead mt-2">Some NVT sites sow wheat twice in a season, side by side: an Early series ahead of the
        usual sowing window and the Main series within it. The study uses these same-paddock pairs in NSW and Queensland,
        2008 to 2017, from the NVT records compiled by Newman and Furbank (2021).</p>
    </div>

    <ol class="prose study-steps">
      <li><strong>Pair.</strong> An Early and a Main trial of the same season on the same paddock form a pair, fixed
        before any yield was read: 205 pairs at 46 sites, sown 7 to 52 days apart (median 19).</li>
      <li><strong>Compare.</strong> Each variety grown in both trials gives one difference, Early yield minus Main yield.
        Aggregated across the whole study, this leads to the Average Treatment Effect (ATE) estimation. The same
        differences, aggregated separately for northern and southern paddocks, lead to Conditional ATE (CATE).</li>
      <li><strong>Check the match.</strong> {"{kernR}"}'s permutation test found no evidence of a difference between Early
        and Main trials in nitrogen applied by sowing.</li>
      <li><strong>Negative control.</strong> 82 pairs sown under a week apart differed by 0.05 t/ha (-0.03 to 0.13), so
        part of the gap may come from causes other than time spread.</li>
    </ol>

    <figure class="study-fig">
      <img src={href("/figures/study/study_map_dag.webp")} width="2000" height="768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
        alt="Left, a map of the paired NVT sites in NSW and Queensland, split at 31.5 degrees south: 85 pairs in the north and 120 in the south. Right, a causal diagram: paddock, season and variety affect both the sowing window and trial yield; flowering, frost and heat sit between the sowing date and yield." />
      <figcaption class="muted">Left: sites north and south of 31.5° S. Right: the Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG, Hernán and
        Robins, 2020), simplified for expositional purposes. Paddock, season and variety normally affect both Sowing window
        (the treatment) and Trial yield (the outcome) and are classical confounders. For the purposes of estimating causal
        effect of Early versus Main time of sowing on yield, these factors have been fixed through the matched pairs
        design, hence eliminating their confounding influence. Flowering, frost and heat themselves depend on the date
        and are not adjusted for. The lower arrow in the DAG transmits the date's other effects: establishment, nitrogen,
        and rain before sowing.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="card featured mt-3">
      <p class="prose">On the same paddock and in the same season, the same variety is expected to yield 0.22 t/ha (6% of
        the 3.8 t/ha Main-window mean) more when sown in the Early window than in the Main window. Further, a gain of
        0.31 t/ha is expected in southern NSW, but only 0.12 t/ha in the northern paddocks. For the latter, the 90%
        interval covers zero (-0.06 to 0.30), so a gain there is not established. Utilising the Bayesian posterior,
        {" "}{"{flexyBayes}"} puts the chance of a gain there at 87%.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="study-fig">
      <img src={href("/figures/study/study_results.webp")} width="2000" height="449" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
        alt="Early minus Main yield for all pairs, the north and the south, from flexyBayes, asreml, proxymix and APSIM calibrated with PESTO, with the chance of a gain from flexyBayes: 0.98, 0.87 and 0.99. Right, the value of the gain in dollars per hectare, on average and for a new paddock and season." />
      <figcaption class="muted">Bars and ranges are 90% intervals; shaded curves are {"{flexyBayes}"} posteriors.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose">
      <p><strong>The verdict.</strong> Early sowing gained 0.22 t/ha, worth $58/ha ($15 to $103) at $261/t (ABARES wheat
        gross unit value, study seasons), provided early sowing does not cost extra. The average gain is positive with
        98% probability. For a new paddock and season, which add their own variation, it is 65%.</p>
      <p class="mt-2"><strong>Four estimates.</strong> {"{flexyBayes}"}, the main analysis: 0.22 t/ha (0.06 to 0.39).
        {" "}{"{asreml}"} agrees: 0.23 (0.09 to 0.37). {"{proxymix}"}, without the normality assumption, agrees: 0.21 (0.15
        to 0.28). APSIM, calibrated with {"{PESTO}"}, expected 0.13 t/ha (0.11 to 0.15).</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="code"><div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="kicker">Run it yourself</p>
      <h2>The main analysis in R</h2>
      <p class="prose mt-2">One script re-runs the {"{flexyBayes}"} analysis from the open data, from the pairing to the
        value of the gain. It needs the NVT tables of Newman and Furbank (2021) as DataHub holds them, ABARES Table 6 for
        the price, and the packages arrow, data.table, flexyBayes (with INLA) and readxl. The {"{asreml}"},
        {" "}{"{proxymix}"}, {"{kernR}"} and APSIM steps are not in it.</p>
      <p class="cta-row"><a class="btn btn-accent" href={href(`/code/${fileCode}`)} download>Download the R script</a></p>
    </div>

    <h3>What a fresh run gives, beside the poster</h3>
    <p class="prose muted mt-1">The run below used flexyBayes 0.18.1 on 24 October 2026. Posterior intervals and chances
      come from 4,000 draws and move by about 0.01 t/ha and one percentage point between runs.</p>
    <section class="table-scroll mt-2" tabindex="0" aria-label="Poster numbers against a fresh run of the script"><table class="num t-wide">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Quantity</th><th scope="col">Poster</th><th scope="col">Fresh run</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {compare.map(([q, a, b]) => <tr><th scope="row">{q}</th><td>{a}</td><td>{b}</td></tr>)}
      </tbody>
    </table></section>

    <details class="acc mt-3">
      <summary><span>Show the script</span></summary>
      <div class="acc-inner"><div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0"><pre class="code-block"><code>{code}</code></pre></div></div>
    </details>
  </div></section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>References</h2>
    <ul class="prose refs">
      <li>ABARES (2026). Agricultural commodities: September quarter 2026, statistical tables, Table 6. Australian Bureau of Agricultural and Resource Economics and Sciences.</li>
      <li>Hernán, M. A., &amp; Robins, J. M. (2020). Causal inference: What if. Chapman &amp; Hall/CRC.</li>
      <li>Newman, S. J., &amp; Furbank, R. T. (2021). A multiple species, continent-wide, million-phenotype agronomic plant dataset. Scientific Data, 8, 116. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-021-00898-8">https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-021-00898-8</a> (author correction: Scientific Data, 9, 437, 2022)</li>
      <li>Sparks, A. (2026). read.abares: Read data from ABARES and ABS (R package version 3.0.0). <a href="https://docs.ropensci.org/read.abares/">https://docs.ropensci.org/read.abares/</a></li>
      <li>The VSNi Team (2026). asreml: Fits linear mixed models using REML (R package version 4.2.0.482). VSN International.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
